        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe dedicated `AuditOwnerCreated` function class is the right call \u2014 one small step, wired with `next:`, reading the saved `Owner` via `@Val`. That part is idiomatic and additive. Two structural issues:\n\n1. **`govern: [ transaction ]` on the audit step is wrong altitude.** Per AGENTS.md, `transaction` governance is for *writes* and must be listed on \"every step it covers.\" The audit step performs no persistence \u2014 it only logs. Governing a pure-logging step as a transactional write is misleading to a maintainer (it implies audit participates in the DB write) and is unnecessary. Beyond the structural smell, it also keeps the step inside the open transaction, so the \"created\" audit line is emitted *before* the pipeline commits \u2014 which contradicts the spec's \"on successful create.\" Drop `govern: [ transaction ]` from the `audit` step (keep it on `save`):\n\n   ```yaml\n   audit:\n     class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.AuditOwnerCreated\n     next: respond\n   ```\n\n2. **Method name `service` breaks the codebase idiom.** Every other function class in this project names its single public method with the verb from the class (`validate`, `load`, `apply`, `save`, `respond`). A generic `service` reads as out of place here. Rename it to match the pattern \u2014 e.g. `audit`:\n\n   ```java\n   public void audit(@Val Owner owner) { ... }\n   ```\n\nNeither changes behavior materially, but both are cheap fixes that keep the new step consistent with the surrounding endpoints."
